\section{Introduction}
Evaluating a language model means running it on dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute and effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile hold a history matrix of the scores of $M$ earlier models on $N$ benchmarks. An evaluator facing a new model can afford only $k\ll N$ runs and wants its scores on the other benchmarks. We ask three questions: \emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, \emph{how well} the rest can then be inferred, and \emph{how large} $k$ must be.

Earlier work, which we survey in Section~\ref{sec:related}, reduces the number of \emph{items} inside one benchmark or completes matrices whose observed cells are random. Choosing the $k$ benchmarks is a design problem, to be tested on a model family absent from the history and on models newer and stronger than it; leave-one-model-out evaluation leaves near-identical siblings in the history and makes prediction look easier than it is.

We study the problem with a protocol that holds out whole model \emph{families} (and, separately, later release dates), fits everything on the training models before selecting benchmarks, and compares selectors and predictors on identical targets with family-clustered, paired uncertainty (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}). The method is a known toolkit applied to a new matrix: a factor model with diagonal noise fitted to the history, Gaussian conditioning to predict the unrun benchmarks, and greedy Bayesian A-optimal selection (Section~\ref{sec:method}). We state a closed-form risk for it and test the formula in three tiers (Section~\ref{sec:theory}). We run everything on three complete leaderboard blocks: two overlapping MTEB embedding-result blocks (English tasks; all tasks) and the 57 subjects of HELM MMLU (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}, Section~\ref{sec:experiments}); the main comparison is repeated on MTEB matrices with their missing cells. The evidence is embedding-model results and the subjects of one benchmark; we do not test suites of benchmarks for generative language models, where the dense blocks we assembled have $N<12$ (Appendix~\ref{app:small}).

